1
00:00:01,420 --> 00:00:03,440
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:04,140
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,620 --> 00:00:08,600
My name is Catherine and today we have a lesson that comes actually from a user request.

4
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:09,380
That's right.

5
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:19,940
We had a user request who asked us if we can do something about a purchasing manager, maybe what his job does or what it is to be a purchasing manager.

6
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:22,680
So you'll find out all about that today in this lesson.

7
00:00:22,940 --> 00:00:26,960
It's an upper intermediate about being a purchasing manager and about vendors.

8
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:28,020
So let's check out today's dialogue.

9
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:37,220
Good morning, Angela.

10
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:38,180
How have you been lately?

11
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:39,640
Morning Michael.

12
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:41,460
I've been very busy lately.

13
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:46,560
One of our other vendors is going out of business and I've been searching for a suitable replacement.

14
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:50,160
Well rest assured that you can count on us to be here for the long run.

15
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:51,260
Please sit down.

16
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:52,820
Coffee?

17
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:53,820
No thanks.

18
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:55,260
I've been trying to cut down on the caffeine.

19
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:57,520
I could never do that.

20
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:00,340
I'd be a zombie if I didn't have my morning coffee fix.

21
00:01:01,220 --> 00:01:02,200
Let's get down to business then.

22
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:03,140
Yes.

23
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:09,240
I've come to talk with you about ordering the 8 megapixel cameras for our new MiPhone.

24
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:13,380
The demand for phone cameras is growing and Pear has been falling behind in the market.

25
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:14,520
That's great.

26
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:17,420
I'm glad to hear that Pear has finally jumped on the bandwagon.

27
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:20,700
Right now our contract is for the 5 megapixel cameras.

28
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:22,760
Is Pear still interested in having those?

29
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:23,820
No.

30
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:26,840
We're changing all the cameras to 8 megapixels.

31
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:33,600
We were hoping that by making your company our sole supplier for cameras, we could negotiate a better deal.

32
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:35,120
Surely.

33
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:36,640
Let's get started by drafting a new contract.

34
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:43,040
Alright we're back.

35
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:45,480
So a very interesting job to be a purchasing manager.

36
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:47,500
A lot of responsibility on top of him.

37
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:55,920
And we actually saw some really interesting phrases and words that are related to business, to negotiating, contracts, etc.

38
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:58,340
So let's take a look at some of those now on Language Takeaway.

39
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:05,160
The first word we have is vendor.

40
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:06,680
This is a noun.

41
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:08,480
It means it's a job.

42
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:09,960
It's the name of a person's job.

43
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:11,120
So he is a vendor.

44
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:13,600
And a vendor, what does a vendor do?

45
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:15,700
Well, a vendor sells things.

46
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:21,800
So the verb here is to vend, like a vending machine is that machine that you can buy sodas from.

47
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:25,280
So a vendor is someone who sells things to someone else.

48
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:28,820
Okay so I can say he is a door vendor.

49
00:02:29,420 --> 00:02:30,140
He sells doors?

50
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:31,040
Yeah, that's right.

51
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:35,800
Or let me contact my vendor about the price list for these TVs.

52
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:37,180
Okay, very good.

53
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,780
So we're talking about a vendor who is going out of business.

54
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:46,400
And because he's going out of business, this manager is actually looking for a suitable replacement.

55
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:50,110
So this is an interesting phrase because these two words normally go together.

56
00:02:50,790 --> 00:02:55,530
We say let's find a replacement, okay that's good, but really we want a suitable replacement.

57
00:02:56,050 --> 00:02:58,970
And so first of all, the word suitable means like appropriate or good.

58
00:02:59,190 --> 00:02:59,850
But what about replacement?

59
00:03:00,070 --> 00:03:00,410
What does this mean?

60
00:03:00,570 --> 00:03:03,590
A replacement is something that takes the place of.

61
00:03:03,590 --> 00:03:05,590
It replaces something else.

62
00:03:05,610 --> 00:03:08,390
So for example, we have our vendor who went out of business.

63
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:19,000
So what we are looking now for is another vendor, but not just any vendor, a suitable vendor, one that has the things that we need or the prices that we need.

64
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,400
And in order to replace the one that went out of business.

65
00:03:21,530 --> 00:03:24,270
Right, so we can say this about other things, not just people.

66
00:03:24,550 --> 00:03:31,090
For example, if I break my iPhone, my cell phone, very expensive phone, I'm upset about it.

67
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:33,550
But when I buy a new one, I don't want a cheap one.

68
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:37,640
I want a suitable replacement, one that's about the same as the old one.

69
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:37,770
That's right.

70
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:42,210
So maybe you don't get the exact same one, but you get one that's very similar.

71
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:43,600
Exactly.

72
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:44,600
Okay, very good.

73
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:55,990
So now they're going to talk a little bit about the contract, but they kind of do a little bit of small talk and they're talking about coffee and he says, well, you know, I'd be a zombie if I didn't have my morning coffee fix.

74
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:57,430
I know how that feels.

75
00:03:57,670 --> 00:04:00,530
This is a person who loves to drink caffeine to wake up.

76
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:05,920
And so we talk about a fix as being something that you need.

77
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:10,650
Okay, my coffee fix means the coffee that I drink in the morning to survive.

78
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:11,490
That's right.

79
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:15,130
Okay, so we know the verb to fix is to make something good again, to repair.

80
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:25,990
But in this case, we're taking fix as a noun and we're saying that it's something that you need, like you said, for example, maybe in the morning you need your English fix and so you'd listen to EnglishPod.

81
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:26,510
Exactly.

82
00:04:26,790 --> 00:04:28,850
You need it so much, it's like a drug.

83
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:29,800
That's right.

84
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:30,360
Very good.

85
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:40,820
And moving on, they started to talk a little bit more about the details of the business and he mentioned that they want to make this company their sole supplier for cameras.

86
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:45,180
Okay, so sole means only.

87
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:48,960
But this word supplier is very interesting because it's very similar to vendor.

88
00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:54,940
So it's basically someone, a company maybe, or a person that supplies you with what you need.

89
00:04:55,800 --> 00:04:57,560
All right, so to supply is to provide for.

90
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:06,730
So for example, if I work at a clothing store and I sell lots of nice clothing, I need a supplier to bring me the clothes that I sell.

91
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:08,610
Otherwise, where am I going to get them from?

92
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:09,550
I don't have a factory.

93
00:05:10,120 --> 00:05:11,120
That's right.

94
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:15,010
For example, IBM is one of the biggest computer suppliers or manufacturers in the world.

95
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:15,610
All right.

96
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:17,320
Okay.

97
00:05:19,700 --> 00:05:23,500
And for the last part of the dialogue, they said, well, I think we can reach an agreement.

98
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:26,680
Let's get started by drafting a new contract.

99
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:28,520
They're going to draft a contract.

100
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:32,660
All right, so this is interesting because we don't use the verb to write a contract.

101
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:38,120
Now you would think, well, of course you write it, but there's a special verb that we use with the word contract.

102
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:44,900
So just remember this phrase together, to draft a contract or my company will draft a contract and send it to you.

103
00:05:45,320 --> 00:05:48,970
This means to prepare a contract in order for someone to sign it.

104
00:05:49,310 --> 00:05:49,750
That's right.

105
00:05:50,030 --> 00:05:56,050
So it's basically the same as write, but it's usually used more with contracts to draft a contract.

106
00:05:56,330 --> 00:05:56,610
Exactly.

107
00:05:56,930 --> 00:05:59,790
Or maybe you can say to draft up a business plan.

108
00:05:59,990 --> 00:06:00,210
Exactly.

109
00:06:00,550 --> 00:06:02,830
So to draft a plan, to draft a contract.

110
00:06:03,090 --> 00:06:07,510
This is basically to write something that has many different points, many different strategies.

111
00:06:07,970 --> 00:06:08,750
Okay, very good.

112
00:06:09,290 --> 00:06:11,110
So five key words for you there.

113
00:06:11,520 --> 00:06:17,640
And now let's move on to some key phrases on Fluency Builder.

114
00:06:17,640 --> 00:06:17,900
Fluency Builder.

115
00:06:19,350 --> 00:06:23,190
Okay, so there are a lot of really, really juicy phrases in today's lesson.

116
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:26,000
The first one is rest assured.

117
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,870
So we heard this from Michael at the beginning of the dialogue.

118
00:06:29,110 --> 00:06:31,670
He said, rest assured that you can count on us.

119
00:06:32,050 --> 00:06:32,530
That's right.

120
00:06:32,770 --> 00:06:33,650
Rest assured.

121
00:06:33,930 --> 00:06:42,230
So this is a phrase that you use to try to give confidence to someone, telling them you can trust me, rest assured that I'll be there.

122
00:06:42,430 --> 00:06:43,210
Don't worry.

123
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:44,760
Don't worry.

124
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:45,410
So let's hear another example of it.

125
00:06:45,960 --> 00:06:48,830
Rest assured, Marco, I will be there to support you.

126
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:52,790
Okay, so I know that you will be there or I can trust that you'll be there.

127
00:06:52,950 --> 00:06:53,210
Okay.

128
00:06:53,510 --> 00:06:53,930
Very good.

129
00:06:55,160 --> 00:06:56,280
So to rest assure.

130
00:06:56,280 --> 00:07:03,210
Now the second part of it is in that same sentence, well, rest assure that you can count on us.

131
00:07:03,750 --> 00:07:07,130
Okay, so what does it mean to count on us, to count on me?

132
00:07:07,390 --> 00:07:15,290
So the verb to count has a couple of different meanings and most of us are familiar with the other one, to count numbers, one, two, three, four, five.

133
00:07:15,880 --> 00:07:25,520
But here to count on someone, remember that preposition on, means to expect someone to do something, to depend on someone.

134
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:25,710
That's right.

135
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:32,590
So if you count on someone, means that you depend on them, you rely on them, you can trust them.

136
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:39,390
So for example, in the sentence that I just told Marco, rest assured, I will be there.

137
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:41,830
You can count on me.

138
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:45,990
That means you can depend on me, you can expect me to be there.

139
00:07:46,130 --> 00:07:47,210
I will not let you down.

140
00:07:47,490 --> 00:07:47,890
That's right.

141
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:49,720
Okay.

142
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:58,360
In that same sentence, we have another phrasal verb, rest assured that you can count on us to be here for the long run.

143
00:07:58,360 --> 00:07:58,970
The long run.

144
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:00,360
All right.

145
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:01,630
So we're not actually running here.

146
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:04,730
The long run is a phrase that means for a long time.

147
00:08:05,510 --> 00:08:13,290
So for example, if I'm in an interview and someone asks me, well, we're really interested in your resume and we really like you.

148
00:08:13,570 --> 00:08:17,250
We want to know, are you available in the long run, in the long term?

149
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:18,100
That's right.

150
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:23,640
So if you say, yes, I am here for the long run, it means that you are here for a long time.

151
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:24,960
Okay.

152
00:08:25,560 --> 00:08:28,160
And let's move on to our next phrase.

153
00:08:28,860 --> 00:08:35,120
And they're actually, you know, they stopped with a small talk and now they want to get down to business.

154
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:39,840
You hear this phrase all the time because you don't just walk into a meeting and start talking about business stuff.

155
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:42,200
You know, you start with the nice things first.

156
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:42,980
You say, how was your weekend?

157
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:43,820
How is your wife?

158
00:08:44,760 --> 00:08:45,580
How was that game of golf?

159
00:08:45,880 --> 00:08:53,870
So you're talking about nice small talk, like you just said, Marco, but when you want to start talking about numbers or products, things like that, that is called business.

160
00:08:54,210 --> 00:08:57,250
So when you want to start that, you say, let's get down to business.

161
00:08:57,550 --> 00:08:58,010
That's right.

162
00:08:58,760 --> 00:08:59,760
That's right.

163
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:04,330
So it's a phrase to say, okay, now let's start talking about what we came here to do.

164
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:05,970
Start talking about business.

165
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:10,890
And the last phrase that we have for you, it's a very interesting phrase.

166
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:21,170
When they were talking about one of the brands of phones, they're saying, I'm glad to hear that Pear has finally jumped on the bandwagon.

167
00:09:21,760 --> 00:09:27,240
Now, Marco, I wish I could tell you, I knew where this phrase came from, but I don't.

168
00:09:27,240 --> 00:09:28,830
Let's just talk about the word bandwagon first.

169
00:09:29,040 --> 00:09:34,680
I think bandwagon means something that everyone is doing.

170
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:34,820
That's right.

171
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:44,640
So I think if everyone likes the color purple and all of a sudden you like the color purple, then that means you just jumped on the bandwagon.

172
00:09:44,950 --> 00:09:45,250
All right.

173
00:09:45,640 --> 00:09:46,640
I jumped on the bandwagon.

174
00:09:47,050 --> 00:09:50,850
So you think of a wagon as like, before they had cars, they had wagons.

175
00:09:51,150 --> 00:09:53,350
They're wooden and they have wheels and they're pulled by a horse.

176
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:57,120
And so everyone is on there and they're all going somewhere.

177
00:09:57,120 --> 00:09:58,990
And I wasn't on it, but I jumped on the back.

178
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:00,610
That means I joined everyone.

179
00:10:00,990 --> 00:10:02,810
I started to do what they're doing.

180
00:10:03,150 --> 00:10:03,530
That's right.

181
00:10:04,420 --> 00:10:08,520
So I guess you agree with them or you're going towards in the same direction that everyone else is going.

182
00:10:08,520 --> 00:10:09,520
Look at Marco.

183
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:10,070
He just bought an iPhone.

184
00:10:10,520 --> 00:10:11,610
Now he's jumped on the bandwagon.

185
00:10:12,320 --> 00:10:12,510
That's right.

186
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:15,440
So everyone is going in the same direction.

187
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:16,430
Everyone is doing the same thing.

188
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:18,170
Everyone likes the same thing.

189
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:23,550
So to jump on the bandwagon, you can use it in the business sense.

190
00:10:23,730 --> 00:10:33,110
For example, if somebody is not agreeing with you or somebody doesn't want to support your project, you can say, I would really appreciate it if you jumped on the bandwagon and supported us on this.

191
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:33,860
That's right.

192
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:39,600
So it's not always a bad thing, but remember that the verb that is synonymous with this is to conform.

193
00:10:40,420 --> 00:10:40,600
Okay.

194
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:42,380
To do what everyone else is doing.

195
00:10:42,540 --> 00:10:42,920
That's right.

196
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:43,460
Okay.

197
00:10:43,860 --> 00:10:45,400
So a lot of interesting things here.

198
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:47,800
Let's listen to our dialogue one last time.

199
00:10:57,710 --> 00:10:58,690
Good morning, Angela.

200
00:10:58,970 --> 00:10:59,630
How have you been lately?

201
00:11:00,290 --> 00:11:01,070
Morning Michael.

202
00:11:01,710 --> 00:11:02,950
I've been very busy lately.

203
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:08,840
One of our other vendors is going out of business and I've been searching for a suitable replacement.

204
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:11,610
Well rest assured that you can count on us to be here for the long run.

205
00:11:12,280 --> 00:11:12,710
Please sit down.

206
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:14,280
Coffee?

207
00:11:14,280 --> 00:11:15,280
No, thanks.

208
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:16,710
I've been trying to cut down on the caffeine.

209
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:18,990
I could never do that.

210
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:21,790
I'd be a zombie if I didn't have my morning coffee fix.

211
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:23,650
Let's get down to business then.

212
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:24,590
Yes.

213
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:30,840
I've come to talk with you about ordering the eight megapixel cameras for our new Mi phone.

214
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:34,840
The demand for phone cameras is growing and Pear has been falling behind in the market.

215
00:11:35,680 --> 00:11:35,980
That's great.

216
00:11:36,680 --> 00:11:38,880
I'm glad to hear that Pear has finally jumped on the bandwagon.

217
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:42,160
Right now our contract is for the five megapixel cameras.

218
00:11:42,800 --> 00:11:44,240
Is Pear still interested in having those?

219
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:45,280
No.

220
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:48,280
We're changing all the cameras to eight megapixels.

221
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:55,040
We were hoping that by making your company our sole supplier for cameras, we could negotiate a better deal.

222
00:11:55,280 --> 00:11:55,680
Surely.

223
00:11:56,240 --> 00:11:58,100
Let's get started by drafting a new contract.

224
00:12:02,350 --> 00:12:05,670
So talking about purchasing, it's actually a very interesting position.

225
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:18,760
I think many, or almost all companies, big companies have this position where not only do they have to, maybe if they're in retailing, they have to purchase the goods that they will sell, but also in offices.

226
00:12:18,770 --> 00:12:23,230
For example, who buys the paper, the printers, the copiers, everything for the office?

227
00:12:23,510 --> 00:12:23,790
That's right.

228
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:33,320
This is a very important position, as you say, because this person can save a company a lot of money if the person knows the right vendors.

229
00:12:33,320 --> 00:12:33,500
That's right.

230
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:39,360
If the only vendors you know are selling things at very high prices, then that's not very good for you or for your business.

231
00:12:40,040 --> 00:12:44,420
But if you know all the inside secrets, you know all the good vendors, then maybe you're going to be able to save a lot of money.

232
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:45,060
That's right.

233
00:12:45,440 --> 00:12:48,880
I think this position is also a lot broader than just this.

234
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:54,080
Maybe someone can also help us out on the website, EnglishPod.com.

235
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:54,540
You can explain.

236
00:12:54,780 --> 00:12:57,820
Maybe if you're a purchasing manager, you can tell us what your job entails.

237
00:12:58,080 --> 00:13:00,600
Or if you know a purchasing manager, what does that person do?

238
00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:01,260
That's right.

